%I A400685 #8 Oct 24 2026 17:29:41
%S A400685 1,0,1,36,837,22523,640872,19059049,585459624,18492733223,
%T A400685 597798332385,19701975640607,659979228111078,22415215605019588
%N A400685 a(n) is the number of ways to divide a 2*n X 2*n square into two connected regions of equal area, each having the same perimeter as the square, by a simple lattice path, up to the symmetries of the square.
%C A400685 A cut is a simple path of unit lattice edges with two distinct endpoints on the boundary and no other boundary points. If its length is p and the boundary arcs between its endpoints have lengths s and 8*n - s, the two region perimeters are p + s and p + 8*n - s. Both equal 8*n, so p = s = 4*n; the endpoints are antipodal noncorners. The regions need not be congruent; by definition, each has area 2*n^2.
%C A400685 Normalize the endpoints to (q, 0) and (2*n - q, 2*n), with 0 < q < 2*n. Let v(n) count the normalized cuts, and let f_180(n), f_h(n), and f_v(n) count those fixed by the half-turn and the horizontal and vertical reflections, respectively. Burnside's lemma gives a(n) = (v(n) + f_180(n) + f_h(n) + f_v(n))/4. Here f_v(n) = 0, since a fixed path would lie on the midline, whose length is 2*n. For n = 3 this gives (122 + 20 + 2 + 0)/4 = 36. The same normalization gives the upper bound a(n) <= (2*n - 1)*3^(4*n - 1), since the first step is forced and each of the remaining 4*n - 1 steps has at most three choices.
%C A400685 Let h(n) = (A001850(n) - 3*A001850(n-1))/2 - 2*(n-1)*A000108(n-1), n >= 2. It counts vectors u = (u(1), ..., u(n-1)) with sum |u(i)| = n and entries of both signs. Moving u(i) units horizontally on row i gives a path from the top side to the center; joined to its half-turn image it is a cut, and at most two such cuts lie in one symmetry class. This gives the first lower bound in the formula section. These cuts are invariant under the half-turn, so their parts are congruent, and they are also counted by A400686. If u and w have equal sums sum u(i) and sum i*u(i), joining the path of u to the half-turn image of the path of w gives an equal-area cut; at most four such cuts lie in one symmetry class, and the Cauchy-Schwarz inequality gives the second lower bound.
%H A400685 Felix Huber, <a href="/A400685/a400685.pdf">Illustration of a(3) = 36</a>.
%H A400685 Felix Huber, <a href="/A400685/a400685.txt">Maple program</a>.
%H A400685 Eric Weisstein's World of Mathematics, <a href="https://mathworld.wolfram.com/Cauchy-FrobeniusLemma.html">Cauchy-Frobenius Lemma (Pólya-Burnside lemma)</a>.
%H A400685 Eric Weisstein's World of Mathematics, <a href="https://mathworld.wolfram.com/SchwarzsInequality.html">Schwarz's Inequality (Cauchy-Schwarz inequality)</a>.
%F A400685 For n >= 2, a(n) >= max(ceiling(h(n)/2), ceiling(h(n)^2/(4*(n - 1)*(2*n*(n - 1) + 1)))), where h(n) = (A001850(n) - 3*A001850(n-1))/2 - 2*(n-1)*A000108(n-1).
%F A400685 a(n) <= (2*n - 1)*3^(4*n - 1), n >= 1.
%F A400685 a(n) >= A400686(n).
%F A400685 Conjecture: lim_{n->infinity} a(n)^(1/n) exists.
%e A400685 a(0) = 1: the empty cut is counted.
%e A400685 a(1) = 0: the only interior vertex is the center, so a path with both ends on the boundary and no other boundary points has length 2, not 4.
%e A400685 a(3) = 36: the square has side 6 and perimeter 24. Each cut has length 12 and splits the boundary into two arcs of length 12, and each region has area 18. There are 36 classes under the symmetries of the square (see the linked illustration).
%p A400685 # See Huber link.
%Y A400685 Cf. A000108, A001850, A068392, A113900, A348456, A400686.
